 ## Add it to Pi
 
-Place the skill in your user or project skills directory. Directories containing `SKILL.md` are discovered recursively.
+Place the skill in your user or project skills directory to add it to the catalog. Directories containing `SKILL.md` are discovered recursively.
 
 Pi also supports the Agent Skills locations `~/.agents/skills/` and `.agents/skills/`. Project `.agents/skills/` directories are discovered from the working directory through its ancestors, stopping at the repository root when one exists.
 
 Pi accepts some standalone Markdown skills, but a directory containing `SKILL.md` is the portable form and should be preferred. See [Settings](settings.md#resources) and [Pi Packages](packages.md) for additional locations.
 
+Catalog sources are considered in this order; name collisions keep the first skill found:
+
+- Global directories: `~/.pi/agent/skills/` and `~/.agents/skills/`
+- Project directories, only after the project is trusted: `.pi/skills/` and `.agents/skills/` in the working directory and its ancestors
+- Packages: `skills/` directories or `pi.skills` entries in `package.json`; project packages require trust
+- Settings: the global `skills` array and, when trusted, the project `skills` array; entries may be files or directories and use package-manager precedence within this group
+
 Project skills can instruct the model to run scripts or modify files. Review unfamiliar skills and their supporting files before granting project trust.
 
+## Activate skills
+
+A catalog skill enters the system prompt only when it is active. Active skills come from:
+
+- Session/CLI: `--skill <path>` (repeatable, additive even with `--no-skills`) or `/skills add <name> --session`
+- Global: `~/.pi/agent/skills.json`
+- Repository: `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json`, keyed by git repository identity
+
+Package- and settings-provided skills that previously loaded automatically must now be enabled in `~/.pi/agent/skills.json` or the applicable entry in `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json`. Use `pi skills list` to find catalog skill names.
+
+Enable global skills with `~/.pi/agent/skills.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "enabled": [
+    "~/.agents/skills/code-review-and-quality",
+    { "source": "~/.agents/skills/browser-testing-with-devtools" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Enable repository-scoped skills with `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "github.com:earendil-works/pi": {
+    "enabled": ["~/.agents/skills/pi-development"]
+  },
+  "local:~/Documents/repos/private-tool": {
+    "enabled": ["~/.agents/skills/private-tool"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Repository keys are resolved from git remotes in this order: `upstream`, then `origin`, then the first configured remote. Remote URLs are normalized so `git@github.com:earendil-works/pi.git`, `https://github.com/earendil-works/pi`, and `ssh://git@github.com/earendil-works/pi.git` all become `github.com:earendil-works/pi`. Non-default ports stay part of the key. If no remote exists, Pi falls back to `local:<repo-root-relative-to-$HOME>`, such as `local:~/Documents/repos/private-tool`.
+
+Manage activation with either the interactive `/skills` command or the one-shot CLI:
+
+```text
+/skills active
+/skills list
+/skills search browser
+/skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --session
+/skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+/skills remove browser-testing-with-devtools --global
+/skills reload
+
+pi skills active
+pi skills list
+pi skills search browser
+pi skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --global
+pi skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+pi skills remove browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+```
+
+`--session` is interactive-only and lasts until the session ends. `--global` and `--repo` persist to their respective JSON configuration. `list` and `search` show catalog skills. `pi skills active` shows the configured global and repository entries; `/skills active` also shows session entries.
+
+Use `--no-skills` to suppress global and repository active skills; explicit `--skill` paths still load.
